<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isaiah Berlin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1909">1909</a>–<a href="/years/1997">1997</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Isaiah Berlin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Isaiah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> was a British social and political theorist, philosopher and historian of ideas, born into a Russian-Jewish family in Riga in 1909. As a boy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> he witnessed the Russian Revolution, and in 1921 his family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. From 1957 to 1967 he was Chichele Professor of Social and Political Theory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>; his inaugural lecture, Two Concepts of Liberty (1958), set negative liberty, the absence of interference, against positive liberty, self-mastery. He became the founding president of Wolfson College, Oxford, in 1966, and his work on liberal theory and value pluralism, and his opposition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, has remained influential.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isaiah-berlin.jpg" alt="Isaiah Berlin" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rob C. Croes (ANEFO) · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IsaiahBerlin1983.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Isaiah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> was born in Riga on 6 June 1909, the only son of Mendel Berlin, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> trader whose family firm was one of the largest in the Baltics, and Marie Volshonok. He witnessed the February and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolutions</a> of 1917 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a>, and a memory of a man dragged from a rooftop by a crowd left him, he said, with a permanent horror of violence. The family left Petrograd in October 1920 and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in early 1921, when he was 11. His English was virtually nonexistent at first, but he reached proficiency within a year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> was educated at St Paul's School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and at Corpus Christi College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, and took first-class honours in literae humaniores in 1928. In 1932, aged 23, he became the first unconverted Jew elected to a prize fellowship at All Souls College. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he worked for British Information Services in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and then at the British embassies in Washington and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> until 1946.</p><p>From 1957 to 1967 he was Chichele Professor of Social and Political Theory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>. In 1966 he helped create Wolfson College, becoming its founding president, and he was president of the British Academy from 1974 to 1978. He disliked writing, and much of his published work comes from recorded lectures, edited from 1974 chiefly by Henry Hardy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His inaugural lecture, Two Concepts of Liberty (1958), defined negative liberty as the absence of coercion by an external body, and positive liberty as self-mastery. He argued that positive liberty had been used in the 19th century to defend nationalism, paternalism and social engineering at the expense of negative liberty. His value pluralism held that moral values may be equally valid and yet incompatible, so that liberty may clash with social justice.</p><p>The Hedgehog and the Fox, his best-known essay for the general public, divides thinkers into hedgehogs, who see the world through one idea, and foxes, who draw on many; he later said he meant it as an enjoyable game. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> on 5 November 1997, aged 88.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Isaiah Berlin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Isaiah Berlin</b>, in lectures given in 1965 and published as The Roots of Romanticism in 1999, called it the greatest single shift in the consciousness of the West.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Since the late 20th century liberals have been divided, with Michael Walzer, <b>Isaiah Berlin</b>, Charles Taylor and David Miller holding that a liberal society needs a stable nation-state.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Riga, in what is now <a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a>. Died in Acland Hospital, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.95,24.11&amp;year=1966" aria-label="Riga on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Isaiah Berlin"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4726 5249h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4726 5249h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.95,24.11&amp;year=1966">What was here in 1966? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
